//! Repro: yo-self ignores the `Acyclic` marker trait when deciding
//! `needs_cycle_gc`, so a program TS compiles with the LIGHTWEIGHT RC runtime
//! gets the full cycle-GC runtime instead.
//!
//! Measure on the emitted C (`--emit-c --skip-c-compiler`):
//!   TS      -> grep -c __yo_gc_collecting = 0   (lightweight, type_id dispatch)
//!   yo-self -> grep -c __yo_gc_collecting = 9   (full Bacon-Rajan cycle GC)
//!
//! Cause: TS `canTypeFormRcCycle` (src/types/utils.ts:1959) returns false when
//! `typeImplementsAcyclic(type, env)`. yo-self's `can_type_form_rc_cycle`
//! (types/utils.yo:759) has NO such check — it goes straight to the field walk.
//! The only RC type here is Mutex, and std/sync/mutex.yo:23 declares
//! `impl(__YO_THREAD_SYNC_TYPE, Acyclic())`.
//!
//! `needs_cycle_gc` is a WHOLE-PROGRAM flag, so one wrong `true` switches the
//! entire emit to the heavy runtime. Two consequences:
//!   1. correctness — the full-GC constructor path stamps
//!      `header.dispose_fn = (void(*)(void*))yo_id_NNNN`, and for these types
//!      that resolves to the BARE GENERIC id (no `_rtparam…` suffix), which is
//!      never emitted => "use of undeclared identifier". This is the
//!      "dispose_fn referencing a never-emitted drop method" class blamed for
//!      the ordered-collections family.
//!   2. performance — the full-GC `__yo_decr_rc` is markedly heavier than the
//!      lightweight one (TLS reads + possible-root buffering per decrement).
//!
//! Porting note: `type_implements_acyclic` lives in
//! evaluator/trait_checking.yo, while `can_type_form_rc_cycle` is in the
//! foundational types/utils.yo, which deliberately avoids evaluator imports.
//! Use the established function-slot indirection (the `g_size_of_type_fn` /
//! `g_alignment_of_type_fn` pattern in that same file) rather than adding the
//! import.
open(import("std/fmt"));
{ Mutex } :: import("std/sync/mutex");

main :: (fn() -> unit)({
  m := Mutex(i32).new(i32(5));
  println(`ok`);
});

export(main);
